(define (problem fleet-corridor)

  ;; Two survey robots at the dock. It is common knowledge that neither is at
  ;; the junction and that neither knows whether the corridor is blocked.
  ;;
  ;; Note what the initial state does *not* say: whether the corridor is
  ;; blocked. The theory leaves it open, so the initial model designates two
  ;; worlds rather than one — genuinely undetermined, the way a corridor nobody
  ;; has looked at is. That is what makes the solution branch.

  (:domain robot-fleet)

  (:requirements
    :equality :list-comprehensions
    :finitary-S5-theories :modal-goals :knowing-whether
    :multi-pointed-models
  )

  (:agents r1 r2)

  (:init
    (:and
      (:forall (?i - agent)
        ([C. All] (not (at-junction ?i))))
      (:forall (?i - agent)
        ([C. All] (<Kw. ?i> (blocked))))
    )
  )

  ;; The whole fleet must know whether the corridor is passable, because both
  ;; robots route around it. Knowing *whether*, not knowing *that*: a mission
  ;; that only succeeds when the corridor turns out to be blocked is not a
  ;; mission, and a plan for it would be unexecutable half the time.
  ;;
  ;; This is the goal that makes the fleet talk. Either robot could drive to
  ;; the junction and look, but looking is semi-private — the other sees it
  ;; look without learning what it saw — so one robot sensing does not inform
  ;; the other. Both could go and look, at four actions; one can go, look and
  ;; broadcast, at three. The planner finds the second, which is to say it
  ;; works out that talking is cheaper than everybody going to see for
  ;; themselves.
  (:goal
    (and
      ([Kw. r1] (blocked))
      ([Kw. r2] (blocked))
    )
  )
)
